  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q1-6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p11-stem1-6-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bob makes the following probability claims. Explain why each conclusion is not justified.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q1",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p11-q1-statement",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A bag contains red, blue, green and yellow marbles. A marble is drawn at random. The probability of drawing a red marble is ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "0.25",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The numbers of marbles of each colour are not given. Red marbles need not make up one quarter of the bag.",
          "worked": "For a random draw, $P(\\text{red})=\\dfrac{\\text{number of red marbles}}{\\text{total number of marbles}}$. Four possible colours do not make the colours equally likely. The probability is $0.25$ only if red marbles make up one quarter of the bag.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q2",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p11-q2-statement",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "There are 26 letters in the alphabet. A random letter from a paragraph is chosen. Therefore, the probability of it being ‘R’ is 1 in 26.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Letters in a paragraph need not occur equally often. The probability depends on the proportion of letters that are ‘R’.",
          "worked": "Randomly choosing a letter occurrence from the paragraph gives $P(\\text{R})=\\dfrac{\\text{number of occurrences of R}}{\\text{total number of letters}}$. The 26 letter types need not be equally likely, so $\\dfrac{1}{26}$ is not justified.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q3",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p11-q3-statement",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A digit is selected from a randomly chosen postcode. There are ten possible digits, ranging from 0 to 9 inclusive, so the probability of any digit being chosen is ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "0.1",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Ten possible digit values do not make them equally likely. Digits can occur with different frequencies in postcodes.",
          "worked": "The rule $P(\\text{one outcome})=\\dfrac{1}{|S|}$ applies only when the outcomes are equally likely. Postcode digits need not have equal frequencies, so ten possible digit values do not justify probability $0.1$ for each.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q4",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p11-q4-statement",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "There are more office workers in Town ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "A",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " than office workers in Town ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "B",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ". Therefore, you are more likely to meet an office worker in Town ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "A",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " than in Town ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "B",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Compare the proportions of office workers, not just their numbers. The towns’ populations are not given.",
          "worked": "For a randomly encountered resident, the relevant probability is $\\dfrac{\\text{number of office workers}}{\\text{town population}}$. Town $A$ can have more office workers but a smaller proportion of office workers than Town $B$, so the conclusion is not justified.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q5",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p11-q5-statement",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "There are four teams ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "A",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "B",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "C",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " and ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "D",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " playing Rugby. Therefore, the probability of Team ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "A",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " winning is ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "0.25",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The four teams need not be equally likely to win.",
          "worked": "Four possible winning teams give probability $\\dfrac{1}{4}=0.25$ for each only when all four are equally likely to win. Their strengths may differ, so Team $A$’s probability is not established.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q6",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p11-q6-statement",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The probability of getting a three when rolling a die is 1 in 6. I have rolled a die ten times and none of them were three. Therefore, my next roll is more likely to land on the number three.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Die rolls are independent. The probability of a three on the next roll remains $\\dfrac{1}{6}$.",
          "worked": "The next die roll is independent of the previous rolls. Ten rolls without a three do not change the next-roll probability: $P(\\text{three on the next roll})=\\dfrac{1}{6}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      }
    ]
  },
